;; llvmi's C++ modules (cpp.samples.N: test/cpp/samples/N.cpp, cpp.runtime.N: test/cpp/runtime/samples/N.cpp) whose outcome is
;; worked out by hand from the program's own source (n-llvm-indep, LLS49 (c), the user 2026-11-18; test/c/selfref-derived.edn's form,
;; n-c-last): the status and the exact stdout from C++23 and Arbace's target (x86-64 psABI, musl 1.2.6, libstdc++ 15's texts), never
;; from a compiler's run. The sources stay as they are (the pack rt's bases, C++'s own records): no self-check line is written into
;; them. arbace.llvm.check's own verdict reads it; an abort's :stderr is libstdc++'s verbose terminate handler's message
;; (vterminate.cc: the type's name and what()) after what the program wrote.
;; llvm-judge-1120 (2026-11-20; doc/llvm/LLVM-CROP-SURVEY.md, "As built: llvm-judge-1120"): a module whose IR fixes another outcome than
;; its source's own expectation has its outcome worked out from the IR itself (:from :ir), a reviewed golden output as Java's
;; test/java/golden (JCS361 (a)) and Haskell's (queue 325g) are: the IR read instruction by instruction, each printed line's basis in
;; :review, never a run's output copied; :ir the reviewed module's SHA-256 (test/llvm/ir/c.tsv's), so a pack made again by another clang
;; has no outcome from it (lli's record judges it until it is reviewed again). `bin/llvmi derived-check` holds every entry to the
;; recorded run (ref-lli23.edn; an abort's stderr g++'s), no module run. Read with the Clojure reader.
[{:id "c-O0.synth.pp-has-attribute" :from :ir :ir "324e8666627a1e55498f9977d75186f47d1d66cdeddf3d201e05eae742fa0708" :status 0
  :stdout "a0 = 0\na1 = 0\na2 = 0\na3 = 0\na4 = 0\na5 = 0\na6 = 0\na7 = 0\na8 = 0\na9 = 1\na10 = 1\na11 = 1\nin_if = 1\n"
  :review [["a0 = 0" "@.str.1 c\"a0\", @a0 = internal global i32 0"] ["a1 = 0" "@.str.2, @a1 = internal global i32 0"]
           ["a2 = 0" "@.str.3, @a2 = internal global i32 0"] ["a3 = 0" "@.str.4, @a3 = internal global i32 0"]
           ["a4 = 0" "@.str.5, @a4 = internal global i32 0"] ["a5 = 0" "@.str.6, @a5 = internal global i32 0"]
           ["a6 = 0" "@.str.7, @a6 = internal global i32 0"] ["a7 = 0" "@.str.8, @a7 = internal global i32 0"]
           ["a8 = 0" "@.str.9, @a8 = internal global i32 0"] ["a9 = 1" "@.str.10, @a9 = internal global i32 1"]
           ["a10 = 1" "@.str.11 c\"a10\", @a10 = internal global i32 1"] ["a11 = 1" "@.str.12, @a11 = internal global i32 1"]
           ["in_if = 1" "@.str.13 c\"in_if\", @in_if = internal global i32 1"]]
  :why "main (optnone) makes 13 calls printf(@.str = \"%s = %d\\0A\", @.str.N, load @aK) in the order a0 ... a11, in_if, then ret i32 0: status 0, nothing on stderr; the globals are internal and main's only store is to its own retval alloca, so each load reads the initializer (%d of an i32 in decimal); 96 bytes. clang 23.1.4 answered __has_c_attribute with the C2x drafts' dates (201904 ...), so each `== 202311L` of a0-a8 was false and the IR holds 0 there, unlike the C source's own expectation (test/c/synth/pp-has-attribute.expect, C23's 202311: 1 each); the module is the test, so its outcome is the IR's"}
